{"tool_name":"align-paths","contents":["oeb","bioschemas","biotools"],"fetched_metadata":{"biotools":{"id":"align-paths","home":"https://github.com/BiodataAnalysisGroup/align-paths","license":"MIT","summary":"Inference of Pathway Decomposition Across Multiple Species Through Gene Clustering.","addition_date":"2017-09-09T17:07:03Z","last_update_date":"2024-11-24T16:01:47.808573Z","tool_type":["Desktop application"],"version":["1.0"],"accessibility":"Open access","biotools_curie":"biotools:align-paths","cost":"Free of charge","credit":[{"email":"fpsom@certh.gr","name":"Fotis E. Psomopoulos","orcidid":"https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0222-4273","typeEntity":"Person","typeRole":["Primary contact"]}],"documentation":[{"type":["General"],"url":"https://github.com/BiodataAnalysisGroup/align-paths/blob/master/README.md"}],"edit_permission":{"type":"private"},"function":[{"operation":[{"term":"Pathway or network prediction","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_3439"}]}],"maturity":"Mature","name":"Align-paths","owner":"fpsom","publication":[{"doi":"10.1142/S0218213015400035","metadata":{"abstract":"In the wake of gene-oriented data analysis in large-scale bioinformatics studies, focus in research is currently shifting towards the analysis of the functional association of genes, namely the metabolic pathways in which genes participate. The goal of this paper is to attempt to identify the core genes in a specific pathway, based on a user-defined selection of genomes. To this end, a novel algorithm has been developed that uses data from the KEGG database, and through the application of the MCL clustering algorithm, identifies clusters that correspond to different layers of genes, either on a phylogenetic or a functional level. The algorithm's complexity, evaluated experimentally, is presented and the results on three characteristic case studies are discussed.","authors":[{"name":"Mitkas P.A."},{"name":"Ouzounis C.A."},{"name":"Psomopoulos F.E."},{"name":"Vitsios D.M."}],"date":